# Brifle SDK

A TypeScript SDK for the REST API of the Brifle platform (`@brifle/brifle-sdk`).

```bash
npm install @brifle/brifle-sdk
```

```ts
import { ApiV1 } from "@brifle/brifle-sdk"

const api = ApiV1.sandbox            // or ApiV1.production, or new ApiV1("https://…")
const login = await api.authentication().login({ key: API_KEY, secret: API_SECRET })
if (login.isError) throw new Error(login.error?.message)

const sent = await api.content().sendContent(TENANT_ID, {
  to: { email: "max@example.org" },
  type: "letter",
  subject: "Ihre Rechnung",
  body: [{ type: "application/pdf", content: pdfBase64 }],
})
console.log(sent.data?.id)
```

Every method returns an `ApiResponse<T>`: `isSuccess` / `data` on success,
`isError` / `error` (`{ code, message, status }`, see the API's error code table)
otherwise. Methods do not throw on API errors; the only exception is the
deprecated `content().getCoverLetterContent` (use `getCoverLetter`).

| Resource | What it covers |
|---|---|
| `authentication()` | `login`, `logout` |
| `content()` | send letters, receiver checks, documents, actions, delivery status and certificate, cover letters, paper-mail preview |
| `contracts()` | documents signed by one or more signers in the Brifle app (needs an API with contracts enabled, otherwise code 40499) |
| `wallet()` | issue, read and revoke wallet items |
| `mailbox()` | inbox and outbox search |
| `tenants()`, `accounts()`, `address()`, `status()` | lookups |
| `signature()` | deprecated, use `contracts()` |

## How the SDK is built

```
openapi/api.json        public API spec, exported from brifle-core (mix openapi.export)
src/generated/          low-level client, generated from the spec (npm run generate), internal
src/mappers/            translate between the public types and the wire format
src/endpoints/, src/api the public API: hand-written, stable
```

The public classes and types are the contract with SDK users. The generated
layer follows the API; the mappers absorb API changes, so a regenerated client
does not change what users compile against.

## Compatibility

Within a major version the public API only grows:

- no method, parameter, type or field is removed or renamed;
- new parameters are optional; request types only get optional fields or wider unions;
- something that has to go is marked `@deprecated` and keeps working until the next major.

This is enforced in CI:

- `etc/brifle-sdk.api.md` (API Extractor) is the reviewed public API. A change
  to it fails CI until the report is updated (`npm run api:update`) and committed,
  so every change to the public API shows up in the pull request diff.
- `test/types/compat.test-d.ts` checks that everything that compiled against the
  published 0.3.3 (`test/compat/baseline/v0.3.3.d.ts`) still compiles.
- `test/types/drift.test-d.ts` checks the public types against the generated
  ones: every declared field exists in the spec and every field the API requires
  can be sent.

## Development

```bash
npm ci
BRIFLE_CORE_DIR=../brifle-core npm run spec:pull   # or SPEC_FILE=/path/to/api.json
npm run generate      # regenerate src/generated from openapi/api.json
npm run typecheck     # compile, compatibility and drift checks
npm test              # offline unit tests (mocked HTTP)
npm run api:check     # public API unchanged? (api:update to accept a change)
npm run build         # dist/ (ESM, CJS, bundled .d.ts)
```

`npm run test:live` runs the old end-to-end tests against a real API. It reads
`API_TEST_ENDPOINT` (default internaltest), `API_TEST_KEY`, `API_TEST_SECRET`,
`API_TEST_TENANT_ID`, `API_TEST_ACCOUNT_ID` and the `TEST_RECEIVER_*` variables
from the environment or a local `.env`.

Releases are made by CI from `release/X.Y` branches, see `.github/RELEASE.md`.
